n)\ndescription: Fragmentation method used for MS/MS.\ninstructions: Use PSI-MS terms. If multiple methods, return a list.\n  Common methods:\n    HCD → NT=HCD;AC=MS:1000422\n    CID → NT=CID;AC=MS:1002472\n    ETD → NT=ETD;AC=MS:1000598\n    EThcD → NT=EThcD;AC=MS:1002678\n    UVPD → NT=UVPD;AC=MS:1003247\n    stepped HCD → NT=HCD;AC=MS:1000422 (stepped HCD is still HCD)\n\nterm: collision energy\nsdrf_column: comment[collision energy]\nontology: MS:1000045\ndescription: Collision energy used for fragmentation.\npattern: {value} {unit} where unit is NCE, %, or eV.\n  Stepped HCD: {value} {unit};{value} {unit};{value} {unit}\n  Examples: "30 NCE", "30% NCE", "27 eV", "25 NCE;27 NCE;30 NCE"\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: dia method\nsdrf_column: comment[dia method]\nontology: PRIDE:0000450\ndescription: Specific DIA method variant (only relevant for DIA datasets).\ninstructions: Return null for DDA XL-MS datasets (which is nearly all XL-MS).\n  If DIA is used, specify the variant (e.g. "SWATH", "MSe", "diaPASEF").\n\nterm: fragment mass tolerance\nsdrf_column: comment[fragment mass tolerance]\nontology: PRIDE:0000576\ndescription: Fragment ion mass tolerance for the database search.\npattern: number + ppm or Da (e.g. "20 ppm", "0.02 Da")\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. Typical XL-MS values: 10–20 ppm for\n  Orbitrap instruments, 0.02–0.05 Da for ion trap.\n\nterm: precursor mass tolerance\nsdrf_column: comment[precursor mass tolerance]\nontology: PRIDE:0000575\ndescription: Precursor ion mass tolerance for the database search.\npattern: number + ppm or Da (e.g. "10 ppm", "5 ppm")\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. Typical: 5–10 ppm.\n\nterm: technical replicate\nsdrf_column: comment[technical replicate]\nontology: MS:1001808\ndescription: Identifier for the technical replicate (integer).\ninstructions: Describe the technical replication strategy found in the text\n  (e.g. "2 technical replicates per sample", "no technical replicates stated").\n  Actual integers are assigned per-file automatically.\n\nterm: data file\nsdrf_column: comment[data file]\nontology: MS:1003083\ndescription: Name of the raw data file (.raw, .wiff, .d).\ninstructions: This is always auto-derived from the PRIDE file list.\n  Describe the file naming convention you observe (e.g. "sample_condition_rep.raw").\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nThe PRC1 and PRC2 proximal interactome\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nPolycomb Repressive Complexes PRC1 and PRC2 play a crucial role in silencing lineage-specific genes during early embryogenesis. To provide new insights in polycomb biology, we profiled the proximal interactome (proxeome) of the catalytic subunits RNF2 (PRC1) and EZH2 (PRC2) in mouse embryonic stem cells (mESCs). This revealed >100 proteins proximal to PRC2 and PRC1, which mainly comprise transcription factors, transcriptional regulators and RNA binding proteins. Interestingly, the EZH2 proxeome included both PRC complexes, while the RNF2 proxeome only identified PRC1 subunits. More than half of the PRC2 proximal proteins are shared with PRC1, revealing the molecular constitution of polycomb chromatin domains. We identified several pluripotency-associated transcription factors, including NANOG, for which we confirmed genomic co-localisation with PRC2. Upon PRC2 disruption, NANOG redistributes to specific sites containing its DNA binding motif. Finally, we compared PRC2 proximal interactomes between naïve mESCs, serum-cultured mESCs and embryoid bodies, altogether providing a comprehensive resource in different cellular contexts that may help to further decipher Polycomb biology.\n\n[METHODS]\n"Proximity labeling experiments Cells were treated with 50mM D-Biotin (Invitrogen) for 1 hour, harvested by trypsinization, washed with PBS and incubated in nuclear isolation buffer (15 mM TRIS, pH 7.5, 15 mM NaCl, 60 mM KCl, 5 mM MgCl2, 1 mM CaCl2, 250 mM Sucrose, 0.03% NP-40) for 30 min at 4 °C on a roller. Nuclei were then pelleted for 10 min (3200 G) at 4 °C, resuspended in 2 pellet volumes RIPA lysis buffer (25 mM TRIS, 7.5 pH, 150 mM NaCl, 1% NP-40, 1% Sodium deoxycholate, 0.1% SDS, 0.5 mM DDT, 1x Protease Inhibitor Cocktail (Sigma-Aldrich) and incubated for 30 min at 4 °C on a roller. Nuclear lysates were then sonicated for 5 cycles (30 sec on, 30 sec off) and centrifuged for 2 min (14000 ×\u2009g) at 4 °C to isolate the nuclear extract (NE) fraction. For affinity purification, 7.5 μL streptavidin-coated beads (Cytiva GE) were washed 3x in RIPA lysis buffer and incubated with 700 μg (Serum; 2i) or 1 mg (EB) NE, ethidium bromide (10 mg/mL) and RIPA lysis buffer in a final volume of 400 μL on a rotating wheel for 3 hours at 4°C. The beads were pelleted at 2000 ×\u2009g for 2 min at 4°C and washed 2x with RIPA buffer, 2x with 1x PBS + 1% NP-40 and 2x with 1x PBS. Beads were incubated in 50 μL elution buffer (2 M Urea, 100 mM Tris, pH 8, 10 mM DTT) on a shaker for 20 min (1250 RPM) at RT. Iodoacetamide (IAA; 50 mM) was added to the beads followed by incubation for 10 min on a shaker (1250 RPM) at RT in the dark. Trypsin (2.5 μL; 0.1 mg/mL) was added and beads were incubated for 2 hours on a shaker (1250 RPM) at RT. Beads were pelleted for 2 min (1500 ×\u2009g) at RT and supernatant containing peptides was collected in separate tubes. Beads were incubated with an additional 50 μL elution buffer, incubated on a shaker at 1250 RPM for 5 min, pelleted and supernatants combined with supernatants from the previous step. Supernatants were incubated with an additional 0.1 μL trypsin overnight at RT for peptide digestion. Digested peptides were acidified with 1% TFA to halt digestion. Samples were desalted on C18 stage-tips as described previously (Rappsilber et al., 2007). Briefly, stage-tips were prepared and washed with methanol followed by washes with buffer B (80% acetonitrile and 0.1% formic acid) and buffer A (0.1% formic acid) twice. All washes were done at 1500 ×\u2009g for 4 min at RT. Samples were loaded onto the stage-tips and centrifuged at 600 ×\u2009g for 10 min at RT and washed once with buffer A. StageTips were stored at 4°C. For each sample, three technical replicates were analyzed using LC-MS. Nuclear extraction and GFP pulldown Cells were harvested with trypsin, washed twice with PBS, and centrifuged at 400 x g for 5 min at 4°C. Cells were swelled for 10 min at 4°C in five volumes of Buffer A (10 mM Hepes-KOH, pH 7.9, 1.5 mM MgCl2, 10 mM KCl), and then pelleted at 400 x g for 5 min at 4°C. Cells were resuspended in two volumes of Buffer A plus protease inhibitors and 0.15% NP-40 and transferred to a Dounce homogenizer. After 30-40 strokes with a Type B pestle, the lysates were spun at 3200 x g for 15 min at 4°C. The nuclear pellet was washed once with PBS, and spun at 3200 x g for 5 min at 4°C. The nuclear pellet was resuspended in 2 volumes Buffer C (420 mm NaCl, 20 mM Hepes-KOH, pH 7.9, 20% v/v glycerol, 2 mM MgCl2, 0.2 mM EDTA) with 0.1% NP-40, protease inhibitors, and 0.5 mM DTT. The suspension was incubated with rotation for 1 h at 4°C, and then spun at 18000 x g for 15 min at 4°C. The supernatant was removed. The nuclear pellets remaining after nuclear extraction were solubilized by resuspension in four volumes of RIPA buffer (150 mM NaCl, 50 mM Tris pH 8.0, 1% NP-40, 5 mM MgCl2, 10% glycerol) plus benzonase (Millipore) at 1000U per 100 μL nuclear pellet. Samples were incubated at 37°C with shaking until solubilized, then spun at 18000 x g for 1